5. Monolithic vs Microservices architecture

→ Monolithic: All major modules run as one deployable application.
→ Microservices: Business capabilities are split into independently deployable services.
→ Monoliths are simpler to develop and deploy initially.
→ Microservices support independent scaling and deployment but introduce network failures, distributed transactions, and operational complexity.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

6. What are the advantages of microservices?

→ Independent deployment of services.
→ Independent scaling based on workload.
→ Clear ownership of business functionality.
→ Better fault isolation when designed correctly.
→ Trade-off: More complex monitoring, security, communication, and data consistency.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

7. How do you migrate a monolith to microservices?

→ First, identify business boundaries and tightly coupled modules.
→ Extract one suitable module into a separate service.
→ Introduce APIs or events and use the Strangler pattern to migrate functionality gradually.
→ Monitor the new service, compare results, and keep rollback options.
→ Avoid migrating everything at once; move incrementally.

