11. What are Spring Bean scopes?

→ Singleton: One bean instance per Spring IoC container.
→ Prototype: A new instance whenever the container is asked for the bean.
→ Request: One instance per HTTP request in a web application.
→ Session: One instance per HTTP session.
→ Application: One instance per ServletContext.
→ WebSocket: One instance per WebSocket session.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

12. What is the Singleton scope used for?

→ Singleton is the default Spring bean scope.
→ It is suitable for stateless services such as StudentService or NotificationService.
→ Spring creates and manages the bean instance.
→ I avoid storing request-specific mutable data in singleton fields because multiple threads may access the same instance.

