15. Java Streams: Count each word in a string.

Input:

String ss = "paragrah epam ubs paragrah epam ubs paragrah epam ubs prasad";

Approach:

→ Split the string into words.
→ Group identical words using groupingBy().
→ Use counting() to count occurrences.

Code:

Map<String, Long> map = Arrays.stream(ss.split("\\s+"))
    .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(
        Function.identity(),
        Collectors.counting()
    ));

→ Function.identity() returns the word itself as the grouping key.
→ Note: Java uses Function.identity(), not Function.Identity().

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

16. Count words across multiple sentences.

Approach:

→ Store all sentences in a List<String>.
→ Convert each sentence into an array of words using split("\\s+").
→ Use flatMap(Arrays::stream) to combine all words into one stream.
→ Group the words and count their occurrences.

Code:

List<String> list1 = new ArrayList<>();
list1.add(ss);
list1.add(ss1);

Map<String, Long> wordCounts = list1.stream()
    .flatMap(sentence -> Arrays.stream(sentence.split("\\s+")))
    .filter(word -> !word.isEmpty())
    .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(
        Function.identity(),
        Collectors.counting()
    ));

→ flatMap() converts multiple streams of words into one stream.
→ The List must be created using new ArrayList<>(); a String cannot be assigned directly to List<String>.

